#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1587e39e-c7bf-5385-b726-a1b497f7c772" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744326d3-8cda-5c1b-a873-11a80a3cee26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301368} {:answer/id #uuid "10204bc6-0d56-5f90-a2fc-bf6e941c8e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369} {:answer/id #uuid "5169bfee-5661-58d7-907e-756bce4f828b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301370} {:answer/id #uuid "7555d86b-df29-5b74-8a6d-108780eeef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301371} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2fcbbe-4aee-52f0-a538-36843a4f6b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f2fcbbe-4aee-52f0-a538-36843a4f6b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301372} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba077f9d-6cfd-54ac-80f8-1aa066f66081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126c871e-695d-54d3-8a27-1a009fd3b7dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117338" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038821/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884a8bc6-2153-5324-9674-77d113cabe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301375} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f76996-b987-5b4c-be48-8fa8d25a2d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301376} {:answer/id #uuid "1293b599-e383-521b-baf8-c2d67023339f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301377} {:answer/id #uuid "17bd9af2-5a00-506c-add5-bb1f361bd05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301378} {:answer/id #uuid "ee780c7e-4fd0-5914-a6e6-ebcf46378d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "884a8bc6-2153-5324-9674-77d113cabe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c301dfb-b9c0-53fd-a0c9-f0adb0313e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c87fb501-fa8f-570c-af52-809859c824eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-117366" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f061aef-c3cc-59a4-8be8-b6da5789c1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301382} {:answer/id #uuid "7eada3b6-cb43-5f7e-8ab1-ee3bdd42cc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301383} {:answer/id #uuid "8a31bd1a-95d4-5553-a904-219978f22b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301384} {:answer/id #uuid "6849c656-103c-5cbc-8ee3-3669895845cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301385} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a0c2ef-0a50-5f07-b4a9-674ffff968ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eada3b6-cb43-5f7e-8ab1-ee3bdd42cc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301383} :answer-field/id #uuid "d17a735b-b5cf-54cd-ae19-a117e3f945cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3728d7d4-f4c7-5690-9736-e69533d0a6ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-117371" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$\n- $(0,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d0ffb3-c274-5957-9de8-6b860acbbd7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108684} {:answer/id #uuid "a859f75b-ba7d-567a-a4b7-320edbe56d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/i